---
import { ArrowLeft } from "@lucide/astro";

import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="Methodology | TrialDiff">
  <main class="page-shell stack methodology-page">
    <a class="button" href="/">
      <ArrowLeft size={16} />
      Back to dashboard
    </a>

    <header class="panel panel-pad">
      <p class="eyebrow">Methodology</p>
      <h1>How TrialDiff ranks amendment history</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        TrialDiff treats severity as review priority. It uses structured JSON Patch data from ClinicalTrials.gov
        history payloads, deterministic path rules, value signals, and timing context to decide which amendments deserve
        human attention.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="panel panel-pad method-section" id="critical-density">
      <h2>Critical Density Denominator</h2>
      <p>
        Dashboard critical density is computed as <strong>critical events per amendment patch</strong>, not critical
        events per classified event. The denominator is total adjacent patches for the trial.
      </p>
      <p>
        This means trials with high administrative-only amendment volume show lower density than trials where most
        amendments yield material changes. The dashboard also applies a <code>patch_count &gt;= 10</code> floor to reduce
        small-denominator distortion.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="panel panel-pad method-section">
      <h2>Rule Taxonomy</h2>
      <p>
        The classifier starts with deterministic JSON Pointer rules over the ClinicalTrials.gov schema. Outcome,
        design, intervention, eligibility, status, timeline, enrollment, and adverse-event paths are mapped to review
        categories and base severities. Each event stores the rules that fired, the rule set hash, and source
        provenance.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="panel panel-pad method-section">
      <h2>Timing Modifier</h2>
      <p>
        Base severity is computed first, then timing context can escalate sensitive changes. For example, an endpoint or
        eligibility change during late recruitment or after completion can carry higher review priority than the same
        change before recruitment begins. The stored event keeps both <code>severity_pre_timing</code> and final
        <code>severity</code>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="panel panel-pad method-section">
      <h2>Dual Distribution Finding</h2>
      <p>
        Amendment volume and critical density answer different questions. Patch volume measures operational activity.
        Critical density measures how consequential those amendments are. Heavy-amendment platform protocols can have
        low critical density, while smaller trials can have a high share of consequential changes.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
